{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The problem asks for 'the single mechanism that BEST explains why the night-time gap is LARGER in some cities than in others.' The target identifies 'stored sensible heat' / thermal mass as the dominant nocturnal mechanism and then says its cross-city magnitude 'scales with built density and impervious surface fraction.' But built density and imperviousness are proxies for how much heat a city *stores during the daytime* — not independent cross-city modulators of the *night-time* gap. The target's own countercondition concedes the actual mechanism: if anthropogenic heat emissions dominate the nocturnal balance, stored heat is secondary. The most cited single cross-city modulator of the *nocturnal* UHI gap is reduced longwave radiative loss via the sky-view factor (street canyon geometry), not thermal mass. By treating 'denser = more massive reservoir' as the cross-city modulator, the target implicitly makes the night gap a residue of daytime storage rather than identifying the single mechanism that specifically governs the *night-time* differential across cities, which the problem demands.","premise":"The strongest single cross-city modulator of the nocturnal UHI intensity is reduced sky view / canyon geometry reducing longwave cooling, whereas the target attributes cross-city variation to daytime thermal-mass storage capacity.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"000000000000","quote":"The cross-city magnitude of this gap scales with built density and impervious surface fraction, as denser, more massive urban fabrics retain more heat per unit area."}],"counterexample":null}]}